WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.520
It’s got to be pretty hard to top a villain
quite so devious and terrifying as Thanos,

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.100
but that doesn’t mean it can’t be done.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:11.140
From death gods to demons to dictators and
beyond, here are the Marvel villains who could

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.060
give the Mad Titan a run for his money.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.740
Sure, Thanos killed half the universe, but
he did it in the pursuit of what he saw as

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.740
a higher purpose.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.120
Hela doesn't need a reason to kill: death
is who she is and what she does.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:30.189
“I am Hela, Odin's first-born, commander
of the Legions of Asgard, the rightful heir

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:32.400
to the throne, and the goddess of death.”

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:37.560
When she kills you, you go to her realm of
Hel, which is 'Hel with one L' if you're keeping

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:42.320
track, and become part of her undead army,
which, as afterlives go, doesn't sound particularly

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:42.820
fun.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.640
This is why Vikings try so hard to prove their
value as warriors, because only the bravest

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.360
and best are chosen by the Valkyrie to spend
eternity feasting in Odin's great hall of

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:52.880
Valhalla.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.240
In theory, everyone else goes to Hel.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.420
The Marvel version of Hela was introduced
by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby in Journey into

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:00.600
Mystery #102.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:06.119
Her intricate armor and multi-horned helmet
are classic Kirby designs, and help make Hela

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.680
one of his scariest-looking characters.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.520
In fact, of all Thor's enemies, she's probably
the scariest.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.280
After all, it's not easy to stop someone from
killing whoever they want when they have dominion

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.280
over the realm of death.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.420
Kang the Conqueror is a strategist by nature.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.660
He never makes a move until everything is
arranged perfectly, and he's confident that

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.760
whatever happens is certain to go his way.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.800
Because he’s from the far future, Kang has
access to time travel, meaning that when he's

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:35.939
orchestrating his manipulative plans, he can
change the past to make the future he wants

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:36.480
more likely.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:41.460
Anytime the Avengers face Kang, they have
to realize that he may have already changed

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.800
their timeline in ways they aren't even aware
of.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:50.540
“I hope you enjoy the rest of your days
on this dead Earth, for this is where I leave

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.040
you.”

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.560
As you might expect, conflicts with Kang can
get pretty confusing.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:01.100
He first appeared in 1964's Avengers #8, but
a previous time-traveling villain named Rama-Tut,

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:05.670
who'd appeared a year before in Fantastic
Four #19, was later revealed to be Kang from

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.140
another place in his timeline.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.910
Thanks to the convoluted nature of time travel,
you never know when more than one Kang might

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:17.470
show up, and at times he may even be at war
with different versions of himself, as happens

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:21.080
in the "Kang War" storyline that started in
Avengers #128.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.360
Mess with Kang and you’ll soon wish you’d
never been born.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.880
Marvel is lousy with demons, but none greater
than Mephisto.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:34.620
Inspired by Mephistopheles from the legend
of Faust, Mephisto has been Marvel's official

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:40.421
Satan stand-in for over 50 years, ever since
his debut in The Silver Surfer #3 by Stan

00:02:40.421 --> 00:02:42.160
Lee and John Buscema.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:47.360
He's the devil who holds Doctor Doom's witch
mother captive in his own version of Hell.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.140
He's also the one who made the deal that turned
Johnny Blaze into Ghost Rider.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.620
Like the demon on which he was based, Mephisto
will show up and say plenty of nice things

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.840
to you if he thinks you might sign on his
dotted line, but if you don't do exactly what

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.840
he wants, you’ll soon find out that he's
more than capable of ruining your life...

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:05.400
and your afterlife.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:12.140
The Hate-Monger was a mysterious villain who
first appeared in Fantastic Four #21 in 1963.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.740
He's an unnerving masked figure in a peaked
hood that immediately brings to mind the Ku

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:21.100
Klux Klan, only he’s also got a ray gun
that makes people hate each other.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.150
He eventually turns out to be none other than
Adolf Hitler, or maybe just one of his doubles

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.160
or clones, who assumed his new identity after
fleeing Germany.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:34.000
This Hitler dies at the end of that first
Hate-Monger story, but it soon turns out that

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.920
hate itself isn’t so easy to kill.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.260
What makes the Hate-Monger scary isn’t just
that he's Adolf Hitler.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.760
The scary thing about the Hate-Monger is the
idea of Hitler and the hate he represented

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.600
being practically impossible to kill.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.840
“There are no men like me.”

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.280
“There are always men like you.”

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:57.000
He just keeps coming back, over and over,
with new and more powerful technology every

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:57.500
time.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.480
Introduced in issue #5 of The Ultimates 2,
the First Firmament is the living embodiment

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.100
of the first universe that ever existed, back
at the dawn of time.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.040
Attacked by the very beings it had created,
its consciousness survived and remained in

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:12.540
the void.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.580
When Eternity, the eighth living universe
to exist, was weakened by other events out

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.260
in the cosmos, the First Firmament returned
and sought revenge.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:26.160
The sheer scale of the cosmic power wielded
by the First Firmament, who has no regard

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.800
for life in our universe, is deeply terrifying
on a cosmic, existential level.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.900
Fortunately, the Ultimates and Galactus were
able to help Eternity defeat the First Firmament,

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.380
but it was doubtless a close call with an
unusually powerful and entirely evil being.

